gp_margins":true,"_score_margins":100.0,"_gate_fund_growth":0.13306839322918226,"_gp_fund_growth":true,"_score_fund_growth":100.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.0,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":false,"_score_shrhldr_yield":11.1,"_gate_share_shrink":0.0021514952411627597,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":47.3,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.0036830073173498134,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":93.9,"_gate_insider_own":0.0539,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":35.9,"_gate_insider_buying":0.0,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":0.0,"pf":["paper"],"description_full":"First Solar, Inc., a solar technology company, provides photovoltaic (PV) solar energy solutions in the United States, France, India, Chile, and internationally. The company manufactures and sells PV solar modules with thin film semiconductor technology that provides conventional crystalline silicon PV solar modules. It also designs, manufactures, and sells cadmium telluride solar modules that convert sunlight into electricity. The company serves system developers, independent power producers, utilities, commercial and industrial companies, large corporate energy buyers, and other system owners and operators. The company was formerly known as First Solar Holdings, Inc. and changed its name to First Solar, Inc. in 2006. First Solar, Inc. was founded in 1999 and is headquartered in Phoenix, Arizona.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Mark R. Widmar | CEO & Director | Age 59 | Compensation: $2.6M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 7K people. Revenue per employee of $661K ranks in the top quartile among Technology peers, with revenue growing 6%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $150K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $2K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (8/10).","financial_summary":["First Solar, Inc. is a large-cap technology business with a $16.1B market cap on $5.2B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +26% over the past three years and +4% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at 19% against a 10% cost of capital \u2014 a 9-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat, with gross margins sitting at 25%.","The business throws off $1.2B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 23 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.35\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and interest coverage of 36\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 5.4% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Elevated short interest (13.8% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting the other way","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Significant insider selling (net $7.5M)","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","dollars":-7452161.95},{"text":"Technology sector lagging the market significantly, underperforming by 9% over 3 months","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Technology ETF trading well below its long-term trend","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Elevated sector volatility (52% annualized) adds uncertainty","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Sector under pressure with a 18.0% decline over the past 6 months","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Antitrust and platform-regulation risk \u2014 the larger the network effect, the larger the regulatory target on its back, and a single consent decree can reset the moat.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"AI disruption of existing software moats \u2014 incumbents with durable margins face commoditisation as AI-native competitors replicate features at a fraction of the R&D cost.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Cybersecurity liability and talent cost inflation \u2014 a single breach can erase years of goodwill, and the war for engineering talent compresses margins even at scale.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Reinvestment rate supports 13% organic growth capacity \u2014 capital is compounding inside the business","Operating margins 24% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Trading at a 59% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","Trading near the bottom of its 52-week range \u2014 the market is offering a discount","Valued at a 62% discount to sector median EV/EBITDA","Piotroski score 7/9 \u2014 financials improving across profitability, leverage and efficiency","Altman Z comfortably in the safe zone \u2014 a fortress balance sheet on this measure","Growing faster than most Technology peers \u2014 taking share inside the profit pool","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"How Is First Solar (NASDAQ:FSLR) Reshaping American Solar Manufacturing?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/energy/how-is-first-solar-nasdaqfslr-reshaping-american-solar-manufacturing","date":"2026-11-26","timestamp":1795711740.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"What Does a Democratic Congress Mean for First Solar (NASDAQ:FSLR) and US Solar Makers?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/energy/what-does-a-democratic-congress-mean-for-first-solar-nasdaqfslr-and-us-solar-makers","date"